    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. Can you go into a bit more detail on the specific chemical mechanism of why higher pH causes the iron and manganese to become insoluble? I want to make sure I describe the precipitation process clearly for the client.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When the pH rises above 6.0, the soil chemistry shifts from a state of \"hungry roots\" to a \"locked vault\" scenario, and the culprit is the hydroxide ion (OH⁻). As the solution becomes more alkaline, the concentration of these OH⁻ ions increases, and they act like tiny, invisible hands reaching out to grab the free-floating iron (Fe³⁺) and manganese (Mn²⁺) ions.\n\nThis is the precipitation process: the iron and manganese ions bond with the hydroxide ions to form insoluble compounds, specifically iron hydroxides and manganese hydroxides. Think of it as a chemical party where the guests (the nutrients) are suddenly forced to wear coats made of rock; they can no longer mingle with the soil solution (the party mix) and are instead deposited as solid particles on the soil surface or within the root zone. These precipitates are chemically stable and inert, effectively rendering the micronutrients unavailable for uptake, much like a goblin hiding a treasure chest under a mountain of boulders.\n\nFurthermore, at higher pH levels, the surface charge of the soil particles changes. Clay minerals and organic matter in the Central Valley",
      "source": "target",
